-- The ways of writing the frequency attributes that both resolvers refuse.
--
-- `@cold` and `@hot` say how often a function runs. They are not inlining directives - the pair is
-- argued in `resolve_mir/decl.h` - and what they have in common with `@inline`/`@noinline` is the
-- two refusals below rather than the meaning.
--
-- Neither takes an argument. There is nothing to qualify: a body either says it is rare or it does
-- not, and a number in the parentheses would be a weight the reader would have to be told how to
-- spend.
@cold(1)
fn measured(x: Int) -> Int = x + 2

@hot(often)
fn qualified(x: Int) -> Int = x + 3

-- The two together are a contradiction rather than a precedence question, so neither applies: a
-- declaration that says two opposite things has stated no intent, and taking the one written first
-- would make the answer depend on the order. `@inline`/`@noinline`'s rule, for its reason.
@cold
@hot
fn undecided(x: Int) -> Int = x + 4

fn main() -> Int = measured(2) + qualified(3) + undecided(4)
